  "result": "No `\\r\\n` sequences appear, and the output is byte-identical to a pure LF script.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe bug: `click.shell_completion.shell_complete()` printed the completion script via `echo(comp.source())`, a plain string written through Python's text-mode stdout. On Windows, text-mode streams translate `\\n` \u2192 `\\r\\n` on write. When shells like msys2's zsh capture that output via `$(...)`, the embedded `\\r` characters corrupt the script and produce `parse error near 'elif'`.\n\nFix: encode the source script to bytes before passing to `echo()` (`src/click/shell_completion.py:47`), which routes it through `echo`'s binary-writer path and bypasses the newline translation, guaranteeing the shell script always uses `\\n` line endings regardless of platform.\n\nVerified with `tests/test_shell_completion.py` (53 passed) and by confirming zsh's `zsh -n` no longer errors on a CRLF-corrupted vs. fixed script.",
